#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265816 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229139 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "68148c93-9139-5deb-8a53-a05d63ec5930" :knowledge-point/title "Using Linearity Properties of Linear Differential Operators"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "55b4ddff-c263-5dbe-b540-8610072861cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263737} {:answer/id #uuid "5ee01579-2851-5ce8-b8b9-ecb5b093cc56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263823} {:answer/id #uuid "b2212b32-79f2-572f-bd4e-74fce4becbfd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264531} {:answer/id #uuid "e5c45080-32dd-5fd6-9485-fb31091cb2f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265002} {:answer/id #uuid "eea0ecf4-e21b-5ca5-9dc1-d0c68c00439c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265088}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5ee01579-2851-5ce8-b8b9-ecb5b093cc56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263823} :answer-field/id #uuid "e471ca41-6d52-5222-9793-59466bf7b5b8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265649}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "bfd36b47-f530-50b4-b4b7-119c298890d2" :question/math-academy-id "q-130080" :question/problem "Suppose that $L$ is a linear differential operator such that $L({y}_{1})=2-{sin}^{2}⁡x$ and $L({y}_{2})=3{cos}^{2}⁡x$ for some functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x).$ What is $L(3{y}_{1}-{y}_{2})?$" :question/worked-solution "For any linear differential operator,\n\n$$\nL(a{y}_{1}(x)+b{y}_{2}(x))=aL({y}_{1}(x))+bL({y}_{2}(x))\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants. \n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(3{y}_{1}-{y}_{2}) & =3L({y}_{1})-L({y}_{2}) \\\\  & =3\\cdot (2-{sin}^{2}⁡x)-3{cos}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =6-3{sin}^{2}⁡x-3{cos}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =6-3({sin}^{2}⁡x+{cos}^{2}⁡x) \\\\  & =6-3 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305140 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229167 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ef7a2bbc-4172-591e-98eb-f7586d3ae06b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution to a First-Order Inhomogeneous ODE With Sinusoidal Forcing"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "577ddd51-e3bd-5790-981c-b2f4d35144fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302989}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "577ddd51-e3bd-5790-981c-b2f4d35144fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302989} :answer-field/id #uuid "d2d672de-2a9b-5e87-8553-d2afe5b67ad3" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304651} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6eb7988a-9925-541c-a1be-439e5577b8e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{19}{24}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303132}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6eb7988a-9925-541c-a1be-439e5577b8e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{19}{24}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303132} :answer-field/id #uuid "f01e1a9b-a805-5e89-ae02-8bb83060bea3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304726}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "51ccf9c3-0db9-5b22-9dee-31cd44f9c3bf" :question/math-academy-id "q-332894" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n4\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+12y=-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+11\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x).\n$$\n\nFind the general solution to this equation given that the complementary solution is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-3x},$ where $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\n$y(x)=A{e}^{-3x}\\,+\\,($\n{{field-1}}\n$)\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,+\\,($\n{{field-2}}\n$)\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)$" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n4\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}+12{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-3x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+11\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x).$ So, we assume the particular solution to be \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n4(-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))+12(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x))=-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+11\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x).\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n(12\\beta +12\\alpha )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+(-12\\alpha +12\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)=-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+11\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x).\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}12\\alpha +12\\beta =-8\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)) \\\\ -12\\alpha +12\\beta =11\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x))\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{19}{24},\\beta =\\frac{1}{8}.$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-\\frac{19}{24}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+\\frac{1}{8}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x).\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-3x}+(-\\frac{19}{24})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+(\\frac{1}{8})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x).\n$$"}]}
